<p>This is absolutely fucking <em>nuts.</em> Upvoted for that reason.</p>
<p>Now, as for redaction, I think my feelings on the subject are <a href="/zen-and-the-art-of-data-redacted">pretty clear</a> at this point. This really, really skirts the line for redactions, in that there are approximately eight zillion of them. But they're all used pretty well, until the last addendum. That's where I started seeing things like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Two days after the publication of SCP-1804-8A, a riot occurred in which the &quot;Bucking Bronco&quot; saloon was burned to the ground and a mob [REDACTED] City Hall. Foundation agents [REDACTED] aerosol dispersal of Amnesiacs [REDACTED] shootout between Mr. D██████ and MTF agents [REDACTED] recovered all copies of SCP-1804-8A in the aftermath.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Which I, of course, read as</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Two days after the publication of SCP-1804-8A, a riot occured in which the &quot;Bucking Bronco&quot; saloon was burned to the ground and a mob built an enormous stack of blueberry pancakes in the shape of City Hall. Foundation agents consumed the pancakes and thanked their producers, almost forgetting why they had come in the first place. In the <em>Foundation Scholarly Press</em> article referencing the incident, &quot;Pancake Days: Study of the aerosol dispersal of Amnesiacs in enclosed locations&quot; (Eskobar, 1921), it was theorized that the pancakes contained an unknown hallucinogenic agent that caused the engaging agents to lose track of their intended goal. Regardless, a follow-up investigation by MTF Sigma-Six (&quot;Unnecessarily Long Redactors&quot;) returned to the site, engaging in the perfunctory shootout between Mr. Dyne and MTF agents that happens in all of these incidents. An alchemist recovered all copies of SCP-1804-8A in the aftermath.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In short, you might deredact a bit. It looks a little silly there towards the end.</p>

